From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 03:22:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08024/11362] Refuse an A/B grid the treatment cannot move: the
 treatment-activation pre-flight

Three A/B measurements on the live ARC agent ran on 2026-07-29 and all three
returned a null. None of the nulls were findings. Diffing the two arms' ACTION
TRACES cell by cell partitions the 12-cell engine-retention grid as:

  6  IDENTICAL        byte-identical traces, both arms complete -- the same agent
  3  TRUNCATION_ONLY  one arm cut off by a wall-clock cap; agreed on every shared action
  2  BOTH_TRUNCATED   neither arm finished; agreed on all 27-28 actions either took
  1  PERTURBED        genuinely different behaviour (vc33, diverging at action 17)

With one perturbable cell the grid's ceiling on discordant pairs is 1, the
sign test needs 6 for p<0.05, and the smallest p it could ever have reported
was 1.0. No outcome of that experiment could have reached alpha. The null was
guaranteed before the first cell started.

REQ-HARNESS-6050 computes that in advance. `preflight_verdict` classifies each
matched pair and REFUSES the grid unless the treatment perturbs enough cells
for the planned test to be ABLE to detect anything. The threshold is DERIVED
from the sign test, not hardcoded. A refusal is a successful outcome: it turns
"we measured it and found nothing" -- which future planners cite as a dead
direction -- into "the treatment never activated, so we have learned nothing
about it yet."

Retrospective validation, which is the only reason to believe it: run against
the 12 committed matched pairs of the grid it was built to catch, it REFUSES
(exit 1, strict ceiling 1, best reachable p = 1.0). A pre-flight that passed a
grid we know was underpowered would be worthless, so that refusal is pinned as
a test rather than reported in prose.

What it is NOT: passing does not mean powered. Perturbation is necessary, not
sufficient -- a treatment can change every action and still bank the same
level count everywhere. It rules out only the dead-on-arrival failure mode.

Also in this commit, each carrying its own review corrections:

* The A/A noise floor is now MEASURED, and the first attempt at one is
  RETRACTED rather than reworded. Pairing the retention grid's `ret1` cells
  against a different experiment's `31b` cells looked like a free A/A ("same
  treatment, seed, model, game") and was not: the held-out arm predates the
  retention treatment by >=6 agentic commits, so the control was an A/B of the
  treatment under test. Its claims are withdrawn from the artifact, the spec,
  the ops docs and the tests, and the invalid pairing is pinned as a test so it
  cannot quietly return -- keyed on the fields the cells RECORD, not on how they
  were configured. A control whose validity rests on an argument is not a control.
  Replaced by a real same-commit A/A at results/arc_goalspec_f9a458e87_preflight_20260730:
  1 of 2 pairs diverges EVEN WITH the sampler seed set, so seeding is NECESSARY
  BUT NOT SUFFICIENT and a floor must be measured per grid. n=2, so no rate is
  asserted. The retention grid's one perturbed cell is therefore UNATTRIBUTABLE,
  not proven-noise; the REFUSE is unchanged either way, so the retraction costs
  a claim and not a decision.

* `LocalGGUFProposer.sampling_seed` closes the root cause: every generation went
  out at temperature 0.2+0.1*attempt with NO seed field, so llama-server picked
  a fresh random one per request and two runs of identical code diverged. The
  harness `seed` seeded random/numpy in the driver and never reached the server.
  Default OFF -- with CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_SEED unset the payload is
  byte-identical to today's, because quietly changing how the scored agent
  samples is a behaviour change, not a measurement change.

* `_guard_engine_write` stops the suite writing results/arc_e3, which is TRACKED
  read-only evidence. test_codeonly_induce_scoping drove an induction without
  redirecting E3_DIR and wrote results/arc_e3/g/world_model.py; it was caught
  only because the stub body happened to be byte-identical to the committed
  content, so git status stayed clean. A different stub would have clobbered
  committed evidence and the next `git add -A` would have committed the clobber.
  Scoped to PYTEST_CURRENT_TEST: the live agent legitimately writes there.

* The artifact-freshness lint gains VERIFIED-INERT acknowledgements, for the
  three registered artifacts that declare a code dependency but no
  rebuild_command. Without a third option the only moves were to edit the
  recorded sha256 silently or to pass --no-verify, and the module's own
  docstring names the latter as the thing to avoid. An acknowledgement pins the
  EXACT new hash plus reason and evidence, so it lapses on the next edit and
  cannot become a standing exemption -- a property exercised during this commit,
  when ruff format moved the module hash and the acknowledgement correctly lapsed.
  The four artifacts that DO have a rebuild command were rebuilt instead; their
  diffs re-pin timing and provenance only, verified field by field against HEAD
  with zero substantive differences, and the three acknowledgement-only
  artifacts are byte-additive.

* The held-out-generalization progress metric resets per level, and the ARC
  no-LLM duration floor is enforced in adversarial_verify.

